## TL;DR

* **`map.loadImage` → returns a Promise**. Use `await` / `.then` instead of a callback.  
* Call it **after the map’s `load` event**.  
* Add the image with `map.addImage('pin', img)`.  
* Once the image is in the sprite, any layer that references `'pin'` will render again.

If you still see errors after applying the fix, double-check:

* CORS headers on the image URL.  
* That you’re not accidentally still using a `mapbox://` sprite or glyph URL in your style.  
* That the style you’re loading (e.g., OpenFreeMap, MapTiler, your own) does not already define a sprite named `'pin'` that conflicts.

Feel free to paste the exact console error (or a minimal reproducible snippet) if something else is still not working!

### What’s happening

In MapLibre GL JS v4 and later **`map.loadImage()` no longer accepts a callback** – it returns a `Promise`.  
Because the callback you passed is ignored, the image never gets added to the style, so any layer that references the image (`icon-image: 'pin'`) ends up with a missing-icon and you see the “callback not being called” message in the console.

The missing marker icons are therefore a direct consequence of the API change, not a sprite-URL problem (your style is already loading, otherwise the map wouldn’t appear at all).

---

## 1️⃣ Convert the `loadImage` call to the Promise form

#### Option A – `async/await` (most readable)

```javascript
async function addPinIcon(url) {
  try {
    const { data: img } = await map.loadImage(url);   // ← returns a Promise
    // `img` is an ImageBitmap (or HTMLImageElement in older browsers)
    map.addImage('pin', img);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Failed to load pin image:', err);
    // handle the error the way you need – e.g. fall back to a default icon
  }
}

// Call it after the map is ready (e.g. in the 'load' event)
map.on('load', () => {
  addPinIcon('https://example.com/pin.png');
});
```

#### Option B – `.then()` chain

```javascript
map.on('load', () => {
  map.loadImage('https://example.com/pin.png')
    .then(({ data: img }) => map.addImage('pin', img))
    .catch(err => {
      console.error('Failed to load pin image:', err);
    });
});
```

> **Why the destructuring?**  
> `loadImage()` resolves to an object `{ data, cacheKey }`. The image bitmap you need is in `data`.

---

## 2️⃣ Verify the layer that uses the icon

Make sure the layer’s `icon-image` property matches the name you gave to `addImage`:

```javascript
map.addLayer({
  id: 'my-pins',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: 'my-points',          // a GeoJSON source you added earlier
  layout: {
    'icon-image': 'pin',        // <-- must be exactly the same string
    'icon-size': 1
  }
});
```

If you still see a blank icon after the `loadImage` fix, double-check:

| Symptom | Likely cause |
|--------|--------------|
| **Icon still missing** | The image failed to load (CORS, 404, or wrong MIME). Open the URL directly in the browser to confirm it returns an image and that the server sends `Access-Control-Allow-Origin:*` (or your domain). |
| **Console shows “Failed to load image …”** | Same as above – fix the URL or enable CORS. |
| **Icon appears but is the wrong size/color** | Adjust `icon-size`, `icon-color`, or the source image itself. |

---

## 3️⃣ (Optional) Pre-load images before the map’s `load` event

If you want the icons to be available **immediately** when the style loads (so they can be used in the style JSON itself), you can add them **before** creating the `Map` instance:

```javascript
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

// Create a temporary map just to get a WebGL context for loading images
const dummy = new maplibregl.Map({ container: document.createElement('div'), style: {} });

await dummy.loadImage('https://example.com/pin.png')
      .then(({ data }) => dummy.addImage('pin', data));

const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty',
  center: [-122.42, 37.78],
  zoom: 12
});

// Transfer the image to the real map once it’s ready
map.on('load', () => {
  const img = dummy.getImage('pin');
  if (img) map.addImage('pin', img);
});
```

> This pattern is only needed when you want to reference the image **inside the style JSON** (e.g., `icon-image: 'pin'` in a layer defined in the style file). For most apps that add the layer programmatically, loading the image after `map.on('load')` as shown in step 1 is sufficient.

---

## 4️⃣ Quick checklist for a clean migration of icons

| ✅ Checklist | What to verify |
|--------------|----------------|
| **Namespace** | All `mapboxgl` → `maplibregl` (including any plugin CSS class changes). |
| **CSS** | If you styled the marker with `.mapboxgl-marker` or `.mapboxgl-ctrl`, rename to `.maplibregl-marker` / `.maplibregl-ctrl`. |
| **`loadImage` usage** | Use the Promise API (`await` or `.then`). |
| **`addImage` call** | Happens **after** the image is successfully loaded and **after** the map is in the `load` state (or after you’ve created a dummy map). |
| **Layer `icon-image` name** | Exactly matches the name you passed to `addImage`. |
| **CORS / URL** | Image URL is reachable, returns a proper image MIME type, and allows cross-origin requests. |
| **Sprite fallback** | If you still want a sprite-based approach, provide a `sprite` URL in the style JSON that points to a sprite sheet you host yourself (MapLibre does not fetch Mapbox sprites). |

