### What’s happening?

1. **`map.loadImage` no longer accepts a callback** –  
   Starting with **MapLibre GL v4.0.0** the method was changed to **return a `Promise`** instead of taking a `(err, image)` callback. Because the callback is ignored, the image never gets added, so any symbols that rely on the image (`icon-image: 'pin'`) disappear.

2. **Your marker icons are missing** because the image you tried to load never made it into the style’s image registry (`map.addImage('pin', …)`).

So the fix is simply to use the promise-based API (or, if you must stay on an older MapLibre v1-v3 branch, keep the old callback style). Below is a quick “cook-book” for both approaches, plus a few extra tips that often cause the same symptom.

---

## 1️⃣ Convert `loadImage` to the promise form

```javascript
// ✅ Modern MapLibre (v4+)
async function addPinIcon(url) {
  try {
    // loadImage resolves with an object { data: ImageBitmap }
    const { data: img } = await map.loadImage(url);
    // addImage expects an ImageBitmap (or HTMLImageElement, Canvas, etc.)
    map.addImage('pin', img);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Failed to load pin image:', err);
    // you can still throw if you want the app to stop
    throw err;
  }
}

// Call it after the map is ready (e.g. in the 'load' event)
map.on('load', () => {
  addPinIcon('https://example.com/pin.png');
});
```

**Or, using `.then()` if you prefer not to make the surrounding function `async`:**

```javascript
map.on('load', () => {
  map.loadImage('https://example.com/pin.png')
    .then(({ data }) => map.addImage('pin', data))
    .catch(err => {
      console.error('loadImage error:', err);
      throw err;
    });
});
```

### Why the `data` property?

`loadImage` resolves to an object `{ data: ImageBitmap }`. The older callback gave you the raw `ImageBitmap` directly, but the promise wrapper adds that tiny envelope. Pull the `data` field (or rename it with destructuring) before passing it to `addImage`.

---

## 2️⃣ If you’re stuck on an older MapLibre version (v1-v3)

The callback signature **still works** on those branches. In that case you can keep your original code, but you’ll have to **pin the package** to a pre-v4 version:

```bash
npm install maplibre-gl@^3   # or 2.x / 1.x as your project requires
```

> **Tip:** The `1.x` compatibility branch is a *drop-in* for Mapbox GL JS v1, but it will not receive new features or bug-fixes. Upgrading to the current v6 (or at least v4+) is strongly recommended for security and performance.

---

## 3️⃣ Common follow-up pitfalls that also hide icons

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Icon shows as a blank square** (or “missing image” in the console) | `map.addImage` was called **before** the map’s `style` was fully loaded, or the image name does not match the `icon-image` value in the layer. | Call `addImage` **after** the `load` event (or after `styledata` if you replace the style later). Ensure the layer’s `layout: { "icon-image": "pin" }` matches the exact name you passed to `addImage`. |
| **CORS error when loading the PNG** | The image URL is on a different origin without proper `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` headers. | Host the PNG on the same domain, enable CORS on the server, or use a data-URI / base64-encoded image. |
| **Sprite-based icons disappear** | You still have a `sprite` URL that points to a Mapbox-hosted sprite (`mapbox://sprites/...`). | Replace the sprite with a self-hosted one (or use the OpenFreeMap style that already bundles a sprite). See the **“Replace the style URL”** section of the migration guide. |
| **`map.addImage` throws “Image already exists”** | You’re adding the same image multiple times (e.g., on every `load` after a style change). | Guard with `if (!map.hasImage('pin')) { map.addImage('pin', img); }` or remove the old image with `map.removeImage('pin')` before re-adding. |

